  "biography": {
    "plain_text": "Norman Bailey Woolworth was an American businessman from the Woolworth retail family. His father, Fred Moore Woolworth, established the chain’s British business, and Norman inherited part of his fortune. He held interests in property and West Indian sugar production, and assembled a collection of American paintings with his wife, Pauline.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 88,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Historical family evidence, independent business context and a contemporary museum catalogue establish the distinct person and activities."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S2",
      "S3",
      "S4",
      "S5",
      "S7"
    ],
    "word_count": 50,
    "html": "<p>Norman Bailey Woolworth was an American businessman from the Woolworth retail family. His father, Fred Moore Woolworth, established the chain’s British business, and Norman inherited part of his fortune. He held interests in property and West Indian sugar production, and assembled a collection of American paintings with his wife, Pauline.</p>\r\n"
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "A talk by Eunice Miner, the New York Academy of Sciences’ executive director, persuaded Norman Woolworth to provide the institution with a permanent home. He donated his Manhattan mansion in 1949. The building gave the academy its own meeting space and remained its headquarters for more than half a century.\n\nWoolworth also shared the paintings he and Pauline collected. After six years of acquisitions, the couple lent a selection to Bowdoin College for an exhibition in 1961. Their collection included landscapes, portraits and still lifes by artists such as Winslow Homer, Mary Cassatt and the Peale family, offering students access to a broad range of American painting.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 96,
      "band": "very_high",
      "reason": "Recipient institution history and the contemporary exhibition catalogue directly establish both contributions."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S6",
      "S7"
    ],
    "word_count": 107,
    "html": "<p>A talk by Eunice Miner, the New York Academy of Sciences’ executive director, persuaded Norman Woolworth to provide the institution with a permanent home. He donated his Manhattan mansion in 1949. The building gave the academy its own meeting space and remained its headquarters for more than half a century.</p>\r\n<p>Woolworth also shared the paintings he and Pauline collected. After six years of acquisitions, the couple lent a selection to Bowdoin College for an exhibition in 1961. Their collection included landscapes, portraits and still lifes by artists such as Winslow Homer, Mary Cassatt and the Peale family, offering students access to a broad range of American painting.</p>\r\n"
  },
